        <h2>What Is a Dead Hang?</h2>
        <p>A dead hang is a static hold from a pull-up bar with arms fully extended and bodyweight supported entirely by your grip. You grab the bar, step off the ground and hang. No pulling. No swinging. Just holding.</p>
        <p>Two versions exist. A <a href="/passive-vs-active-hang/">passive dead hang</a> lets the shoulders relax toward the ears with no shoulder blade engagement. An active dead hang pulls the shoulder blades down and back while keeping the arms straight.</p>
        <p>The passive version stretches the shoulders and trunk. The active version builds shoulder blade stability and prepares you for pull-ups. Both versions train grip endurance. Many programs alternate between the two.</p>

        <h2>Dead Hang Benefits at a Glance</h2>
        <p>Dead hangs are used for seven purposes. Only the grip and mortality association has cohort evidence; the rest are common practice.</p>
        <ol>
          <li><strong>Grip strength:</strong> Support grip and forearm endurance improve with regular progressive hanging.</li>
          <li><strong>Spine stretching:</strong> Hanging unloads the spine while you hang. No study has measured disc height or pressure during a bodyweight hang.</li>
          <li><strong>Shoulder mobility:</strong> Hanging puts the arms overhead, and some people find it eases shoulder tightness. No trial shows it reverses impingement.</li>
          <li><strong>Upper body foundation:</strong> A hang loads the forearms, shoulders and trunk together.</li>
          <li><strong>Posture:</strong> Hanging stretches the chest and front of the shoulder. No study shows lasting posture change.</li>
          <li><strong>Pull-up progression:</strong> Many coaches use about 30 seconds as a rough readiness cue for negative pull-ups. It is a rule of thumb, not a tested threshold.</li>
          <li><strong>Longevity:</strong> Lower grip strength is associated with higher all-cause mortality in cohort data. Grip is a marker, not a cause.</li>
        </ol>
        <p>Read the full breakdown in the <a href="/deadhang-benefits/">dead hang benefits guide</a>.</p>

        <h2>Proper Dead Hang Form</h2>
        <p>Grab the bar with an overhand grip at shoulder width. Wrap your thumbs around the bar. Step off the platform and let your full bodyweight hang. Straighten your arms completely.</p>
        <p>Relax your shoulders for a passive hang or pull the shoulder blades down for an active hang. Tuck your pelvis slightly. Bring your legs together. Breathe slowly and do not hold your breath.</p>
        <p>Common mistakes include bent elbows, forced shoulder shrugging, body swinging and breath holding. Each mistake shortens your hold and can make your shoulders and elbows sore.</p>
        <p>The complete <a href="/how-to-deadhang/">step-by-step dead hang instructions</a> cover grip placement, shoulder position, breathing and dismounting. The form guide and the mistakes guide listed under related guides give cues for every body segment and the most common errors.</p>

        <h2 id="workout-plan">Dead Hang Workout Plan</h2>
        <p>A dead hang workout plan needs three variables: how long you hold, how many sets, and how often you train. The table below gives a common starting plan. Add about 5 seconds to your target hold once you complete every set cleanly, roughly once a week. This is common training practice, not a tested prescription.</p>
        <p class="table-cue">Scroll the table horizontally on smaller screens.</p><div class="table-scroll" role="region" aria-label="Scrollable comparison table" tabindex="0"><table class="data-table">
          <thead>
            <tr><th scope="col">Hold</th><th scope="col">Sets</th><th scope="col">Frequency</th><th scope="col">Rest</th></tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>10 to 20 sec</td><td>2 to 4</td><td>3 sessions a week on non-consecutive days</td><td>60 to 90 sec</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table></div>
        <p>Add a session only if you stay pain free, and leave at least one rest day between hard sessions. Total hang time per session is a useful number to track alongside your longest hold. Keep the form strict, and stop a set one or two seconds before your grip gives out rather than training to failure. The <a href="/deadhang-workout/">dead hang workout routine</a> combines hang sets with grip variations and complementary exercises.</p>

        <h2>Muscles Worked During Dead Hangs</h2>

        <h3>Primary Muscles</h3>
        <p>Your forearm flexors do the most work. The flexor digitorum profundus, flexor digitorum superficialis and flexor pollicis longus sustain your grip against bodyweight. The forearm has about 20 muscles, and the finger flexors do most of the work in a hang.</p>

        <h3>Secondary Muscles</h3>
        <p>In a passive hang the shoulder muscles are mostly relaxed. In an active hang the lower traps, serratus anterior and lats work to hold the shoulder blades down. The posterior deltoids and upper and middle trapezius help keep the shoulder stable, and the rhomboids pull the shoulder blades back during active hangs.</p>

        <h3>Stabilizer Muscles</h3>
        <p>Your transverse abdominis and obliques limit lateral sway. The rectus abdominis resists spinal hyperextension. Gluteal muscles steady the pelvis. Deep spinal erectors maintain trunk alignment.</p>

        <p>A hang loads the forearms, shoulders and trunk together. Learn more about <a href="/grip-strength/">grip strength anatomy</a> and forearm training.</p>

        <h2>Tracking Your Dead Hang Time</h2>
        <p>Track your holds against your own earlier holds. No validated population norms exist for timed dead hangs.</p>

        <p>Published studies on <a href="/dead-hang-time-by-age/">dead hang times by age</a> give context but no reliable percentiles. For the next steps, follow the dead hang progression ladder listed under related guides.</p>

        <h2>Dead Hang Variations</h2>
        <p>The main dead hang variations change grip, shoulder position or load to target different training goals.</p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Passive hang:</strong> Relaxed shoulders for a shoulder and trunk stretch.</li>
          <li><strong>Active hang:</strong> Shoulder blades engaged for shoulder blade control.</li>
          <li><strong>Overhand grip:</strong> The standard grip, which matches the pull-up.</li>
          <li><strong>Underhand grip:</strong> An alternative that puts the biceps tendon under stretch; stop if the front of the elbow hurts.</li>
          <li><strong>Mixed grip:</strong> One hand over and one under, so the trunk works to stay still.</li>
          <li><strong>Towel hang:</strong> A harder hold using a towel over the bar.</li>
          <li><strong>One-arm hang:</strong> An advanced hang that puts the full load on one arm.</li>
          <li><strong>Weighted hang:</strong> External load via a dip belt, added only after a comfortable bodyweight hang of about 45 seconds.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>The full breakdown is in the <a href="/deadhang-variations/">dead hang variations guide</a>.</p>

        <h2>Training Programs</h2>
        <p>Structured programs give you a week-by-week plan. Three program lengths cover different starting points.</p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>4-week program:</strong> Builds toward a 30-second hang from a 15 to 20 second baseline; repeat weeks 3 and 4 if you start lower.</li>
          <li><strong>8-week program:</strong> For a comfortable 20 to 30 second hold.</li>
          <li><strong>12-week program:</strong> For a comfortable 30 second or longer hold; adds load or one-arm work as options.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>Start with the program that matches your current hold. The <a href="/training-programs/">training programs overview</a> helps you choose.</p>

        <h2>Grip Progression Milestones and What They Mean</h2>
        <p>The timeline below is an example for one hypothetical beginner; individual progress varies widely. Tracking progress through milestones keeps motivation high and lets you measure your own change.</p>
        <p><strong>Weeks 1 and 2 (5 to 15 seconds):</strong> Your first hang often feels harder than it looks. Soreness in your forearms after the first few sessions is common. Hold for whatever duration feels sustainable, then rest. Start with 3 sessions a week on non-consecutive days. Stop one or two seconds before your grip gives out and step down under control. Do not hang to failure or drop.</p>
        <p><strong>Weeks 3 and 4 (15 to 30 seconds):</strong> Holds that felt impossible in week one may now feel routine. Many people raise frequency here. Tendons adapt more slowly than muscle, so add sessions one at a time and back off if elbows or fingers ache. Add a second grip variation, such as neutral grip or mixed grip.</p>
        <p><strong>Weeks 5 to 8 (30 to 60 seconds):</strong> At about 30 seconds many coaches use the hang as a rough readiness cue for negative pull-ups; it is a rule of thumb, not a tested threshold. Progress often slows at this stage. When progress stalls, shorten the rest between sets from 90 to 60 seconds, or add a set to your session.</p>
        <p><strong>Weeks 9 to 12 (60 to 120 seconds):</strong> Holds in this range are advanced territory. Weighted dead hangs make sense once you have a comfortable, pain-free bodyweight hang of about 45 seconds, adding load in small steps. Rotate between passive hangs, active hangs, towel hangs and fat-grip hangs to keep training varied.</p>

        <h2>Body Weight Effects and Modifications</h2>
        <p>Bodyweight directly affects hold time because you are supporting your total mass. A heavier person with the same forearm strength as a lighter person will hold for less time. This is physics, not weakness.</p>
        <p>If you are significantly overweight and new to hanging, expect lower initial holds. As your forearm strength improves your hold time can increase even if your bodyweight stays the same. Foot support or a loop band takes part of your weight. Record the assist you used so tests stay comparable.</p>
        <p>Compare your holds with your own earlier holds rather than with other people, because bodyweight changes what a given hold means.</p>

        <h2>Equipment Recommendations</h2>
        <p>Two bar types cover most home setups. Your choice depends on your living situation, budget and training goals.</p>
        <p>See the <a href="/equipment-guide/">equipment guide</a> for the full overview of bars, chalk and grip aids.</p>

        <h3>Doorway Pull-Up Bars</h3>
        <p>Doorway bars install without screws and fit many door frames. They cost less and need no permanent modification. Check the manufacturer's rated load. Best for renters and beginners.</p>
        <p>See the <a href="/doorway-bars/">doorway bar guide</a> for what to check.</p>

        <h3>Wall-Mounted Pull-Up Bars</h3>
        <p>Wall-mounted bars bolt into studs or masonry for maximum stability. They often have higher load ratings and allow wider grip positions. Installation requires drilling. Best for dedicated home gyms.</p>
        <p>See the <a href="/wall-mounted-bars/">wall-mounted bar guide</a> for what to check.</p>

        <h3>Accessories</h3>
        <p>Chalk dries the skin and increases friction between skin and bar. Grip attachments add training variety with fat grips, towels and pinch blocks.</p>
        <p>See <a href="/equipment-reviews/">how the site assesses equipment</a>. The site has not load-tested any bar.</p>

